PIMID: a fixed guest identity, stack limit and program path (-guest-ids, -rlimit-stack, -execfn)

QEMU user mode gives the guest the host user's identity and the host's
limits: getuid and the other identity calls, the auxiliary vector's AT_UID,
AT_EUID, AT_GID and AT_EGID, the owner and permissions of every file the
guest stats, uname, and getrlimit/prlimit64. The guest keeps these values in
its memory (the dynamic linker keeps each library's stat, glibc sizes every
thread's stack from the stack limit), so the same program gave other
simulated results for another user, for files installed with another umask,
and for another ulimit -s. The program path QEMU places on the guest's stack
(AT_EXECFN) is the path it was started with, so the installation path
reached the guest too.

With -guest-ids U:G (or QEMU_GUEST_IDS) the guest sees a fixed identity:
getuid, geteuid and getresuid answer U; getgid, getegid and getresgid answer
G; getgroups answers the one group G; the auxiliary vector carries AT_UID,
AT_EUID = U, AT_GID, AT_EGID = G and AT_SECURE 0; a stat, fstat, newfstatat
or statx result whose owner is the host user (its real or effective id)
reports U, whose group is one of the host user's groups reports G, and, for
a regular file or directory of the host user, the permissions 0755 (a
directory or a file its owner can execute) or 0644 (any other file); files
of other owners, root included, are reported as they are. uname reports the
node name "pimid", the domain name "(none)", the version "#1 SMP" and, unless
-r gives one, the release "6.8.0".

With -rlimit-stack B (or QEMU_RLIMIT_STACK) getrlimit and prlimit64 report a
stack limit of B bytes (soft; the hard limit unlimited); setting it is
unchanged.

With -execfn PATH (or QEMU_EXECFN) the auxiliary vector's AT_EXECFN string
is PATH; QEMU still loads the program from the path it was given. -0 sets
argv[0] the same way.

Without the options QEMU is unchanged.

